WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.858
This lesson covers the CAP theorem, consistency levels, and quorums: the vocabulary behind every distributed storage interview question.

00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:14.469
Picture two bank branches that share account balances over a phone line.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:19.296
One day the line is cut. A customer at branch one wants to withdraw money.

00:00:19.746 --> 00:00:24.669
Branch one can refuse until the line is back, which is consistent but not available.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:30.715
Or it can allow it and risk the other branch allowing it too, which is available but not consistent.

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:36.709
CAP stands for consistency, availability and partition tolerance.

00:00:37.159 --> 00:00:42.337
Consistency here means every read sees the latest write, as if there were one copy.

00:00:42.787 --> 00:00:47.129
Availability means every request to a working node gets an answer.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:54.859
A partition is a network split. Partitions will happen, so the real choice during one is consistency or availability.

00:00:56.209 --> 00:00:58.194
Quorums make the trade off adjustable.

00:00:58.644 --> 00:01:03.683
With N copies, a write waits for W acks, and a read asks R replicas.

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:12.167
If R plus W is greater than N, every read set overlaps every write set, so a read always reaches at least one fresh copy.

00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:19.490
With N equals three, W equals two and R equals two, you can lose one node and still read your writes.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.487
Consistency levels from strongest to weakest.

00:01:23.937 --> 00:01:27.397
Linearizable: behaves like one copy with one clock.

00:01:27.847 --> 00:01:30.749
Read your writes: you always see your own updates.

00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:36.447
Eventual: replicas converge if writes stop, but reads can be stale for a while.

00:01:36.897 --> 00:01:41.564
PACELC adds: even without a partition, you trade latency for consistency.

00:01:42.914 --> 00:01:52.620
During a partition, pick consistency or availability; quorums with R plus W greater than N give overlap. Use this language in every storage design.
